      "message": {
       "content": "# Research Agenda: Reducing Hallucinated Citations in LLM Outputs\n\n## Context and Problem Framing\n\nHallucinated citations are particularly insidious because they carry the *appearance* of authority. A citation that looks structurally correct (author, title, venue, year) but doesn't exist, or misattributes a claim to a real paper, can propagate false information through academic and professional workflows. This agenda is structured as a dependency chain: each experiment's results inform the design of the next.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment 1: Characterizing the Hallucination Landscape\n\n**Goal:** Build a comprehensive benchmark and taxonomy of citation hallucination types, establishing a baseline across models.\n\n### Hypothesis\nHallucinated citations are not a monolithic failure mode. They fall into distinct categories (fabricated papers, real papers with wrong claims, real papers with wrong metadata, plausible-but-wrong authorship) that occur at different rates and are triggered by different prompt structures. A fine-grained taxonomy will reveal which failure modes are most common and which are most dangerous.\n\n### Dataset Construction\n- **Core benchmark (~5,000 prompts):**",
       "name": null,
       "reasoning": "The user is asking for a research agenda to reduce hallucinated citations in LLM outputs. This is a well-defined, important problem in AI research. Let me think through this carefully and propose a structured research agenda with 3-5 concrete experiments.\n\nLet me think about what makes this problem interesting and what approaches would be most impactful:\n\n1. The core problem: LLMs generate citations that look plausible but don't correspond to real papers, or misattribute claims to real papers.\n\n2. Key dimensions:\n- Detection: Can we detect hallucinated citations?\n- Prevention: Can we prevent them at generation time?\n- Retrieval: Can we improve the retrieval-augmented generation pipeline?\n- Training: Can we fine-tune models to be more honest about citations?\n- Evaluation: How do we measure this reliably?\n\nLet me design a coherent research agenda where each experiment builds on the previous ones.\n\n\nI'll structure this as a progression from understanding the problem through detection, prevention, and finally training-based solutions. The first experiment would establish a comprehensive benchmark to measure hallucination rates across different model types and citation styles. Then I'd move into detection methods that can identify hallucinated citations in real-time, followed by prevention strategies that intervene during generation, and finally training approaches that teach models to be more honest about their sources.",
       "reasoning_content": "The user is asking for a research agenda to reduce hallucinated citations in LLM outputs. This is a well-defined, important problem in AI research. Let me think through this carefully and propose a structured research agenda with 3-5 concrete experiments.\n\nLet me think about what makes this problem interesting and what approaches would be most impactful:\n\n1. The core problem: LLMs generate citations that look plausible but don't correspond to real papers, or misattribute claims to real papers.\n\n2. Key dimensions:\n- Detection: Can we detect hallucinated citations?\n- Prevention: Can we prevent them at generation time?\n- Retrieval: Can we improve the retrieval-augmented generation pipeline?\n- Training: Can we fine-tune models to be more honest about citations?\n- Evaluation: How do we measure this reliably?\n\nLet me design a coherent research agenda where each experiment builds on the previous ones.\n\n\nI'll structure this as a progression from understanding the problem through detection, prevention, and finally training-based solutions. The first experiment would establish a comprehensive benchmark to measure hallucination rates across different model types and citation styles. Then I'd move into detection methods that can identify hallucinated citations in real-time, followed by prevention strategies that intervene during generation, and finally training approaches that teach models to be more honest about their sources.",
